# <%= current_page.data.title %>

[Dependabots](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/pulls/app%2Fdependabot) in `modernisation-platform-environments` can be difficult to manage given the volume that occurs in the repository.
A better strategy is to update Terraform modules in batches in each of the Analytical Platform environments in the repository. This can be completed manually or using GitHub Copilot.

> Some changes can be handled as batch updates. Other changes, such as major version updates, are best handled separately from batch updates.

## Manually updating Terraform modules

1. Select an environment, below I've selected `analytical-platform-common` and I'm looking in `iam-roles.tf`
2. Hover over the `source` field for each resource. Hold `Cmd` on Mac and click on the source itself.
3. In this case this redirects to [Terraform Registry](https://registry.terraform.io/modules/terraform-aws-modules/iam/aws/5.59.0)
4. Establish if the current version is the latest version.
5. If not, update it.
6. Iterate through the `.tf` files in each account and update where required/desired.

From here create a PR and interpret the results. Alternatively you can plan locally throughout.

## Using a GitHub Copilot agent

A [GitHub Copilot agent](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/.github/agents/terraform-maintenance.agent.md) has been developed that can carry out the above tasks for you.

- Scans specified environments for Terraform modules
- Identifies outdated module versions
- Updates them to latest safe versions
- Detects breaking changes and skips updates with them unless safe
- Creates pull requests with detailed change summaries

### To run

1. Open a dev container in `modernisation-platform-environments` local repository.
2. Ensure you have GitHub Copilot chat open.
3. Issue the following command in the chat, amending for the relevant component.

```text
@terraform-maintenance update modules in analytical-platform-ingestion
```

> As the agent is still in its infancy you should double check its recommendations, especially any 'safe breaking changes'.

